 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}C \\\\  & =3C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 6 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 6 \\\\ 9 & 6 & 3\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Distributive Law"}
